WEBVTT

NOTE Original complete video/audio are preserved.
Source speech alignment and confirmed corrections are recorded privately.

1
00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.450 position:50% align:center size:90%
My name is Juliana. I am a psychologist,
psychoanalyst and virtues therapist.

2
00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:12.610 position:50% align:center size:90%
I worked in the prison system for many
years, and our goal

3
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:18.550 position:50% align:center size:90%
inside prisons was always to help
prisoners change, but

4
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.330 position:50% align:center size:90%
it was very difficult, because our work
was very limited.

5
00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:29.110 position:50% align:center size:90%
Eduardo Casarotto’s proposal to bring
virtues into

6
00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:34.970 position:50% align:center size:90%
prisons is truly a revolution within those
institutions.

7
00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:41.190 position:50% align:center size:90%
Because making use of this time in
isolation to put

8
00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:46.930 position:50% align:center size:90%
into practice what the Penal Enforcement
Law proposes: a reality

9
00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.670 position:50% align:center size:90%
of transformation.

10
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:54.510 position:50% align:center size:90%
Making use of the time they spend
confined, in isolation,

11
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.110 position:50% align:center size:90%
to really think, right?

12
00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.370 position:50% align:center size:90%
It is not confinement for its own sake,
not just punishment, right?

13
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:06.970 position:50% align:center size:90%
But truly seeking work that brings about
transformation,

14
00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:10.770 position:50% align:center size:90%
a search for self-knowledge, right?

15
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:16.630 position:50% align:center size:90%
Using that isolation to think about
virtues, to improve,

16
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:21.450 position:50% align:center size:90%
right? To work on acceptance, to work on
forgiveness,

17
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:27.250 position:50% align:center size:90%
to reassess themselves, reinvent
themselves, and truly return to

18
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.170 position:50% align:center size:90%
society as a better person, right?

19
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:35.770 position:50% align:center size:90%
So there really is a therapeutic process
within the prison.

20
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.210 position:50% align:center size:90%
And something we notice in the reality of

21
00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:46.010 position:50% align:center size:90%
prisons is that their role is

22
00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.450 position:50% align:center size:90%
much more punitive.

23
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:54.970 position:50% align:center size:90%
Unfortunately, prisons do not have that
capacity to transform

24
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.010 position:50% align:center size:90%
people’s lives.

25
00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:00.990 position:50% align:center size:90%
Even with a social reintegration
department, prisons are

26
00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:01.850 position:50% align:center size:90%
limited.

27
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:06.270 position:50% align:center size:90%
So what Eduardo Casarotto proposes is a
revolution,

28
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:10.670 position:50% align:center size:90%
something unprecedented that must be put
to use.

29
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:16.630 position:50% align:center size:90%
It really is something of tremendous
value.
